(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608125000")
  (:adr/title "A home for the detectors that cannot be gates, and the rule that a standing finding is not a new one")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-12")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2608124800" "ADR-2608121000" "ADR-2608071000" "ADR-2607300900"
                "ADR-2607252000" "ADR-2607178000"))
  (:adr/body
  "## What this builds

ADR-2608124800 established that roughly twenty verification scripts **cannot be
fleet-CI gates**, for a structural reason CLAUDE.md already documents: a fleet
gate ships only the target repo's own tree, so a detector that reads `orgs/`
scans an empty workspace on a node and can never be green there.
`root-permit-index` is 0 pass / 282 fail for exactly this. Two of the detectors
say so in their own headers.

So the answer for them is not a `gates.edn` line. It is a home on a machine that
has `orgs/`. This lands that home:

| file | what it is |
|---|---|
| `scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs` | the runner. Registry-driven, read-only, monitor-not-gate |
| `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` | which detectors, how often, and what each would have caught |
| `.claude/hooks/session-start-orgs-detectors.cljs` | where findings are read |
| `scripts/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick.plist` | scheduling, **deliberately not installed** |

Wired today: `verify-vendored-copies` and `verify-require-graph`, the two the
ADR ranks first.

## The five uninstalled plists, and what pattern to keep from them

Measured 2026-08-12 against `~/Library/LaunchAgents`: of the six plists in
`scripts/`, exactly one — `com.network-awai.hyakka-knowledge-ingest` — is
installed. `com.gftd.tsukuru-maturity-tick`, `com.gftd.fleet-sync-tick`,
`com.gftd.docs-edn-repair`, `com.gftd.industry-stack-wave` and
`com.gftd.kaiyu-kaizen` are not, and three of those five are described by a
CLAUDE.md skill as running every cycle.

Reading them, **the pattern is not why they were dropped.** They are good:
`fleet-sync-tick` states its interval with the measurement behind it (15
manifest entries drifted in the hour after a reconcile), states that it never
writes to main, and states why `ProcessType Standard` rather than `Background`
(measured: a Background+Nice job got 0.09s of CPU in 15 minutes on this
machine). `tsukuru-maturity-tick` documents that launchd cannot answer a
Keychain unlock prompt, so its custody probe records `:unknown` rather than
`:ok`.

What is wrong is one per detector. Each is a separate file that has to be
separately installed, and installing is the step nobody does. So this generalises
the pattern into **one scheduler with a registry**: adding the next detector is
a registry entry, not a sixth plist that also does not get installed. The good
parts of those plists are kept — measured intervals, an explicit statement of
what is touched, `ProcessType Standard` — and the header of the new plist says
what would run, how often, and what it writes, in full.

`com.gftd.tsukuru-maturity-tick` itself is **not** absorbed here. A concurrent
session was assessing it, and its probes need kagi and B2 credentials, which is
the class this home explicitly cannot schedule (below). Its recovery stays its
own decision.

## What the tick does with a finding, which is the whole design

It cannot open PRs on twenty repos. Writing to a log nobody reads reproduces the
failure the ADR is about — `audit-gftd-scripts` was correct and unheard for
weeks. So the tick writes state, and a SessionStart hook puts it where CLAUDE.md
already puts three other hooks that are read.

**The load-bearing rule: a finding that changed must be distinguishable from one
that has been true for weeks.** Four gates here stand at 867, 282, 269 and 268
consecutive failures, and the ADR's conclusion is that a standing red is
indistinguishable from a new red. So each finding carries `:first-seen`, and the
report has three classes:

- **NEW** — first seen in the most recent run. Named, with detail.
- **RESOLVED** — present before, absent now. Named in the run that resolved it.
- **STANDING** — everything else. **One line, a count and the oldest age. Never
  enumerated.** It has already been said.

Four details that are not obvious and were each forced by something measured:

1. **The first successful run is a BASELINE, not N new findings.** On day one
   everything is new; shouting it is the same false alarm from the other
   direction. It keys off `:ok-runs`, counted separately from `:runs` — measured
   during this work: a first attempt died before scanning, consumed the
   baseline, and the real first measurement arrived disguised as 13 NEW.
2. **A finding that comes back is new again.** Its old `:first-seen` is not
   restored. \"Broken for three weeks\" and \"broken again since this morning\"
   are different facts.
3. **RESOLVED is surfaced by run, not by a time window.** A 7-day window would
   make one resolution readable at every session start for a week, which is the
   same noise as a standing red in a happier colour.
4. **The staleness warning has a 30-minute floor**, not just a multiple of the
   interval. Measured on the fixture: without a floor, a short-interval detector
   reports itself stale seconds after running.

## Three floors, because a detector that reports nothing is ambiguous

1. **A run with no evidence is `:inconclusive`, never clean.** Each entry
   declares an `:evidence` regex that must appear in the output; the detectors
   print `SCANNED<TAB>n<TAB>unit` and the regex requires n > 0. ADR-2608124800
   found five scripts that print `PASSED` without measuring; this is the floor
   that stops a sixth being added here by accident. It fired for real during
   this work: a bug in the tick's own env handling made the detector fail in
   0.15 seconds, and it was recorded `:inconclusive` rather than clean.
2. **An `:inconclusive` or `:error` run does not overwrite the finding set.**
   Otherwise a detector that failed to start would report every standing finding
   as RESOLVED — the most dangerous lie this thing could tell.
3. **Admission is fail-closed.** An entry missing `:writes :none`,
   `:timeout-ms`, `:evidence`, `:findings :protocol` or `:why` aborts the whole
   tick with exit 2 rather than being skipped quietly.

## Identity comes from the detector, not from a regex over its prose

Each detector emits `FINDING<TAB>severity<TAB>key<TAB>detail` under `--findings`.
The alternative — the tick scraping the human report — would mis-key every
finding the first time a message was reworded, and **silently report every
standing finding as new**, which is precisely the failure being fixed. The flag
is additive: without it, output and exit code are unchanged.

`severity` separates what gates from what does not. `verify-require-graph` emits
its `:kotoba-orphan` findings as `fail` (these are what make it exit 1) and its
`:sibling-gap` findings as `warn` (report-only, because an un-checked-out
provider looks identical). A NEW `warn` is visible without ever gating on it.

## Proof that it fires

CLAUDE.md: 落ちない gate は劇場. A tick that has never reported anything is
indistinguishable from one that is not running, so it was broken on purpose and
watched.

The breakage is on a **four-repo fixture workspace in `/private/tmp`, not on
real checkouts** — the tick is forbidden from writing under `orgs/`, so breaking
a real vendored copy to test it is not available. Runs, verbatim:

    RUN 1  baseline           vendored: ok exit 0, 0 findings, scanned=1
                              require:  ok exit 0, 0 findings, scanned=4
    BREAK  upstream gains `(pos? (:onchain-confirmations receipt))`; the
           vendored copy keeps the old body.  provider moves problem.cljc ->
           problem.kotoba and leaves the caller.
    RUN 2  vendored: ok exit 1, 1 finding    NEW stale:.../pay/facilitator.cljc
           require:  ok exit 1, 1 finding    NEW kotoba-orphan:kotoba.dsl.problem
    UNBREAK
    RUN 3  vendored: ok exit 0, 0 findings   RESOLVED stale:.../pay/facilitator.cljc
           require:  ok exit 0, 0 findings   RESOLVED kotoba-orphan:kotoba.dsl.problem
    RUN 4  both clean, hook prints one liveness line and nothing else

The two breaks are deliberately the shapes that were actually paid for:
ADR-2608121000's drifted payment-confirmation guard, and ADR-2608071000's
namespace migrated to `.kotoba` with the callers left behind.

Then the claim this whole design rests on, measured directly:

    RUN 5  break re-applied    NEW  kotoba-orphan:kotoba.dsl.problem
    RUN 6  nothing changed     standing 1 件 — not enumerated, not an alarm

The same finding is an alarm exactly once. `--self-test` covers the same
classification as 12 pure assertions, including that a returning finding does
not inherit its old age.

Against the real workspace, baseline run recorded in
`~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/state.edn`:

    verify-vendored-copies   101s   scanned=34     exit 1   13 fail,  0 warn
    verify-require-graph     480s   scanned=3571   exit 1    2 fail, 97 warn

Seven of the 13 are on `pay/x402` and `treasury/core` — the payment paths. The
two `fail` findings from the require graph are `kotoba.dsl.problem` (11 repos,
the ADR-2608071000 breakage, still live 22 days later) and `kotoba.lang.async`
(1 repo). The 97 `warn` are report-only sibling gaps that never gate.

Note the two `scanned` units are not comparable and are not meant to be: 34
vendored *files* against 3571 *repos with a source root*. Each detector declares
its own unit on the `SCANNED` line.

## Read-only, stated at the strength it actually holds

Detectors run with `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=none`, so a `git fetch`/`clone`/`push`
inside one fails at the transport layer rather than succeeding quietly, and with
`GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0` so read commands do not refresh an index. **That is
transport hardening, not a sandbox.** It does not stop a local `git checkout` or
a stray write; those are held off by admission — `:writes :none` declared per
entry and reviewed before it lands — which is weaker, and is written down as
weaker rather than implied.

A stat sentinel over every `orgs/*/*/.git/HEAD` was considered and rejected:
this machine runs many concurrent agent sessions that legitimately move HEADs in
`orgs/`, so it would fire on other people's work constantly, and CLAUDE.md's own
shallow-check hook warns that a guard which false-positives gets ignored, and an
ignored guard protects nothing.

The tick writes only under `~/.gftd/orgs-detector-tick/`. Nothing goes into any
checkout, including the superproject — a tick that dirtied the shared checkout
would feed the stash-churn failure CLAUDE.md documents.

## What this home cannot host, stated rather than implied

- **`annex-custody-verify`** needs B2 credentials. They are on this machine, not
  on a fleet node, so it *can* run here — but **not under launchd**, which
  cannot answer a Keychain unlock prompt (ADR-2607178000, the same wall
  fleet-ci hit and the same one `tsukuru-maturity-tick` documents). Scheduling
  it would produce a permanent `:inconclusive`: another standing red. It belongs
  in the hand-run set.
- **Anything needing `gh`** is rate-limited against one token shared with every
  concurrent agent session on this machine. Not scheduled here.
- **`audit-gftd-scripts`** reads `$HOME/.gftd` — correct here, meaningless on a
  node — but it is cheap and ADR-2608124800 ranks it as a SessionStart hook,
  which is a better fit than a 6-hour tick. Not taken here.

## The plist is not installed

ADR-2608124800 counted five uninstalled plists; shipping a sixth silently would
repeat the finding. The mechanism deliberately does not depend on it: run the
tick by hand and the hook reports it; never run it and the hook says so in the
first line of every session. `scripts/com.gftd.orgs-detector-tick.plist` states
what would run (two detectors, 6h and 24h, ~11 minutes worst case, ~2 minutes
typical), what it writes (three files under `~/.gftd`, nothing in any checkout)
and what it needs (no network of its own, no credentials, no `gh`). **The
scheduling decision is the owner's, and installing it changes only how often the
numbers refresh — not what is reported, where, or to whom.**

## What is not done

The other ~18 ungatable detectors are not registered. Each needs a `--findings`
emit and an entry justified by `:why`, and adding them wholesale would produce
one enormous baseline that nobody reads — the failure mode this is against.
`verify-namespace-collisions` and `verify-duplicate-registrations` are the
ranked next two.

The four permanent reds (867/282/269/268) are still undiagnosed. Three of the
four are root-tree gates and out of this home's scope; diagnosing them remains
ADR-2608124800 item 8.")))
